WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.212
Cryptographic hashes produce one-way digests.

2
00:00:02.212 --> 00:00:10.324
They are useful for identifying data, checking that content has not changed in a trusted context, building higher-level protocols through approved

3
00:00:10.324 --> 00:00:14.011
libraries, and comparing known values without storing the original data.

4
00:00:14.011 --> 00:00:15.486
Hashes are not encryption.

5
00:00:15.486 --> 00:00:18.435
A digest is not meant to be decrypted.

6
00:00:18.435 --> 00:00:25.072
If a system needs confidentiality, it needs an encryption design with keys and access control, not a hash.

7
00:00:25.072 --> 00:00:27.653
Checksums and cryptographic hashes are also different.

8
00:00:27.653 --> 00:00:34.290
A checksum may detect accidental corruption, but it is not designed as a security control against deliberate modification.

9
00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:37.239
Security-sensitive integrity should use approved cryptographic mechanisms.

10
00:00:37.239 --> 00:00:42.401
A message authentication code, or MAC, adds a secret key to the integrity decision.

11
00:00:42.401 --> 00:00:52.356
A valid MAC shows that the message was produced or accepted by a party with the key, assuming the key is protected and the verification is handled

12
00:00:52.356 --> 00:00:52.725
correctly.

13
00:00:52.725 --> 00:00:54.937
Password storage has its own rules.

14
00:00:54.937 --> 00:01:01.574
Passwords should not be stored as plaintext, and fast unsalted hashes are not acceptable for modern defensive systems.

15
00:01:01.574 --> 00:01:07.842
Use approved password-hashing functions or platform identity systems with salts and work factors according to policy.

16
00:01:07.842 --> 00:01:13.373
Avoid building password storage from low-level pieces unless organizational policy explicitly provides the construction.

17
00:01:13.373 --> 00:01:19.272
The application should rely on vetted mechanisms, maintain configuration, and plan for parameter upgrades over time.

18
00:01:19.272 --> 00:01:27.752
Reviewers should ask whether the code is using the right tool: hash for digest identity, MAC for keyed integrity, password hashing for stored

19
00:01:27.752 --> 00:01:29.596
passwords, and encryption for confidentiality.

20
00:01:29.596 --> 00:01:31.808
They should also check comparison behavior.

21
00:01:31.808 --> 00:01:38.445
Security-sensitive comparisons should use approved library routines where policy requires them rather than ad hoc string comparisons.

22
00:01:38.445 --> 00:01:45.819
For password systems, operational controls matter too: account recovery, rate limits, rotation policy, and integration with identity providers can be

23
00:01:45.819 --> 00:01:48.400
just as important as the storage primitive.
